  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "USB grinds out the 2025 Investor Day framework: 4-6% revenue growth as fixed-rate securities and swaps keep repricing into NII (NIM 2.79% in Q2-26, rising), payments (Elavon merchant acquiring, card) and the newly acquired BTIG lift fee income, and expense discipline delivers ~200bp of operating leverage until the efficiency ratio settles near 55-56%. Credit stays benign-to-normalising (NCOs ~0.5-0.6%), CET1 is run flat at ~10.8% and everything above the capital needed for growth goes out as dividends and buybacks. Return on tangible common settles ~19%.",
    "bull": "Rate-neutral balance sheet plus continued asset repricing push NII growth above the range; payments re-accelerates as Elavon's integrated-software channel scales; BTIG and the capital-markets build add a durable fee leg; and the March-2026 capital proposals land 'favorable' as management expects, letting USB hold less capital per dollar of RWA. Efficiency reaches ~53-54%, ROTCE ~21%, and the market pays a premium-regional multiple again.",
    "bear": "A 2027-28 US recession: loan growth stalls, Fed cuts compress deposit spreads faster than assets reprice, card and CRE-office losses push provisions to ~1% of loans, and the efficiency programme stalls under BTIG and technology spend. USB stays well-capitalised (no solvency case -- CET1 10.8%, diversified fee base), but earnings power resets lower and the market caps it like a sub-par regional."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "A bank is valued on what common shareholders can take out: net income to common less the earnings that must be retained to keep CET1 at 10.8% as the balance sheet grows, discounted at the cost of equity. Deposits and wholesale debt are operating funding, so there is no net-debt deduction; preferred dividends and NCI are removed inside the flow.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Credit cycle: provision is ~7.6% of revenue today; a 1% NCO year takes ~$2B pre-tax out of one year's earnings (bear case).",
      "Rate path: NII repricing tailwind assumes the fixed-rate book keeps rolling at higher yields; faster Fed cuts compress deposit spreads.",
      "Capital rules: the March-2026 proposals are 'expected to be favorable' but unquantified; AOCI (-$7.1B) inclusion in CET1 under any revived Basel endgame would raise retention needs.",
      "Retention ratio: CET1/revenue 1.646 is backed out of reported ratios (TCE/RWA 9.4%, CET1 10.8%), not a disclosed RWA figure.",
      "Terminal value ~55-65% of value; the 15x FCFE cap and 3% terminal growth carry it."
    ]
  },
